# BRAND SYSTEM

Two tiers live in this file (`design/DESIGN-AUTHORITY.md`):

- **Tier 1 — Brand identity.** The "communicate / should NOT feel" lists, the imagery concept-not-noun principle, and the design principle at the end. These hold in every task mode. Any redesign must still deliver them.
- **Tier 2 — Current art direction.** Everything from "VISUAL LANGUAGE" through "TYPOGRAPHY" and "CARDS": the specific palette, materials, lighting, type stack and card language that implement the identity today. Mandatory in Maintenance Mode. In Exploration Mode a concept may propose a different implementation of the same identity and must say what it changed and why. Gold as wordmark-only and the Blog/Store palettes are Joe-decided; challenge them only explicitly.

## TIER 1 — IDENTITY
The website represents a premium enterprise managed IT and cybersecurity company.
The visual identity should communicate:
- trust
- competence
- technical sophistication
- security
- reliability
- maturity
- precision
- premium service
The website should NOT feel:
- childish
- gimmicky
- cyberpunk
- hacker-themed
- overly futuristic
- generic SaaS
- AI-generated
- stock-photo driven
- visually noisy
## TIER 2 — CURRENT ART DIRECTION
# VISUAL LANGUAGE
The current visual direction is:
PREMIUM ENTERPRISE TECHNOLOGY
+
DARK TECHNICAL ENVIRONMENT
+
RESTRAINED VIOLET ACCENTS
+
SOPHISTICATED 3D / DIGITAL VISUALIZATION
Think high-end enterprise technology product visualization rather than generic cybersecurity artwork.
## COLOR
Primary environment (keep these **unsaturated** so accents can pop):
- near black `#0a0a0a` (`--de-surface`, marketing field)
- charcoal `#151217` (`--de-raised`, cards / contained boxes)
- graphite `--de-bg` `#050312` (deep well)
- hairline `--de-hairline` `rgba(255,255,255,0.1)`
- paper `--de-paper` `#f7f5f2`
Accent:
- brand magenta `#D3126A` (primary CTA, brand mark, active border, colon, icon)
- deep violet `#5B45E0` / `#7c3aed` — lighting only
- restrained purple `#8B5CF6` / lavender `#A78BFA` — lighting only
**Accent-pop rule:** magenta reads vibrant because boxes and backgrounds stay black/charcoal. Do not fill panels, chips, or section fields with violet/purple. No `#FF00FF`, no rainbow, no purple-wash chrome (`bg-violet-500/15`, indigo→purple CTA gradients).
Homepage chapters must juxtapose this ladder (well / surface / paper / magenta) — do not flatten every section to one `#0a0a0a` slab.
The DE mark is white type + gold 3-bar icon (`#e7b20d`). Gold is secondary only. Do not adopt reference-site yellow as a brand or CTA color.
Executable rule: `.cursor/rules/dark-field-accent-pop.mdc`.
Blog/Journal and Store palettes are locked — `.cursor/rules/blog-store-color-lock.mdc`.
## MATERIALS
Preferred:
- graphite wells and charcoal raised boxes (hairline, not color-filled)
- gunmetal
- smoked glass used sparingly
- dark metal
- subtle translucent materials
- controlled reflective surfaces
Avoid:
- purple-filled chrome (tinted wells, glass-violet cards)
- toy-like plastic
- excessive chrome
- cheap glossy surfaces
- cartoon materials
## LIGHTING
Preferred:
- controlled studio lighting
- subtle rim lighting
- soft violet illumination
- deep shadows
- restrained highlights
- cinematic but clean lighting
Avoid:
- blown-out highlights
- excessive bloom
- giant neon glows
- cyberpunk lighting
- rainbow lighting
## TYPOGRAPHY
Typography should feel:
- modern
- highly legible
- confident
- restrained
- professional
Use hierarchy intentionally.
Do not compensate for weak hierarchy by making everything larger or bolder.
Live type: Space Grotesk (headings), Inter (body), Oxanium / JetBrains Mono (stats).
## CARDS
Cards should feel like part of a coherent interface.
Avoid:
- excessive floating cards
- excessive rounded containers
- random card treatments
- inconsistent border radii
- inconsistent padding
Prefer:
- consistent spacing
- subtle borders
- controlled contrast
- clear hierarchy
- strong internal composition
## IMAGERY (concept-not-noun is Tier 1; the rendering language is Tier 2)
Do not automatically represent concepts literally.
Avoid default imagery such as:
- shield for cybersecurity
- padlock for security
- laptop for IT
- robot for AI/IT
- hacker in hoodie
- generic server rack
- glowing binary code
Instead communicate the underlying concept.
Example:
Managed IT:
centralized infrastructure and orchestration
Co-managed IT:
two complementary systems working together
Risk assessment:
infrastructure being analyzed/scanned
Monitoring:
technical telemetry and system state
Identity:
controlled access architecture
Backup:
redundancy and layered infrastructure
## DESIGN PRINCIPLE (Tier 1)
Every visual element should have a reason to exist.
When choosing between:
"more visual effects"
and
"better composition"
choose better composition.
When choosing between:
"more decoration"
and
"better hierarchy"
choose better hierarchy.
Premium design should feel intentional, not busy.

Visual System v2 (`design/VISUAL_SYSTEM_V2.md`) adds precision, evidence, and interactivity as upper layers on this foundation — not a cyberpunk or Huntress restyle. In Maintenance Mode do not invent HUD chrome ad hoc.
